<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Via Regia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Via Regia, or royal road, was a major medieval trade route across central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, running from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erfurt">Erfurt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorlitz">Görlitz</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wroclaw">Wrocław</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a>. Under royal protection, it carried cloth, dyes, salt and other goods for centuries and enriched the towns along it. It is now promoted as a European cultural route.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/via-regia.jpg" alt="Via Regia" width="320" height="234" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Maximilian Dörrbecker (Chumwa) · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Via_Imperii_und_Via_Regia.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
